---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { groupsEnabled } from '../lib/flags';

const { user } = Astro.locals;
const groups = groupsEnabled();
---

<Layout title="Privacy — Radar" user={user}>
  <h1>Privacy</h1>
  <p>
    This page says exactly what is stored, what leaves the server, and to whom.
    It changes only when the software changes.
  </p>

  {
    groups ? (
      <>
        <h2>Your archive, and groups</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>Your archive</strong> holds everything: the links you save, the page text we
          archive for you, and your short note about why you saved each link. Only you can
          see it unless you share a save to a group.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>A group</strong> you share to sees the link and your note — not your name or
          email, and not who else is in the group. That goes for the group's owner too. Members
          see how many people shared a link, and can export the links with those counts and
          notes. A group never sees your other saves or anything you didn't explicitly share
          to it.
        </p>
        <p>
          The group's owner can remove a link from the group, which removes everyone's share
          of it there. It stays in your archive.
        </p>
      </>
    ) : (
      <>
        <h2>Your archive is private</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>Your archive</strong> holds everything: the links you save, the page text we
          archive for you, and your short note about why you saved each link. Only you can see
          it.
        </p>
      </>
    )
  }

  <h2>What is stored</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Your email address, for sign-in.</li>
    <li>Links you save: the canonical URL, the URL you actually saved, and the page's
      extracted text and a cleaned snapshot, archived so you can search and keep it.</li>
    <li>Your notes and timestamps{groups ? ', and group memberships' : ''}.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>No ad identifiers, no analytics profiles, no payment data{groups
    ? ', no data about where a group member came from'
    : ''}.</p>

  <h2>What leaves the server, and to whom</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>OpenAI</strong> (embeddings and, occasionally, a single categorization
      call): the <em>title and lead text of the public web page</em> you saved — never
      your notes, never your name, never which user saved it. OpenAI's API terms exclude
      training on this data. When you search your archive or save a lens, the words you
      searched for go to OpenAI as well, so results can match by meaning. They aren't sent
      with your name or account.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Internet Archive (Wayback Machine)</strong>: only when you tick "also save
      to Wayback" on a save, we ask it to archive that public URL. Never for pages that
      required a login.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Email provider</strong>: your email address, to send sign-in links. Nothing
      else: no notifications, no digests.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What we will not do</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>No ads, no sponsored links, no affiliate rewriting.</li>
    <li>No training models on your content, ours or anyone else's.</li>
    <li>No selling or sharing data.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Export and deletion</h2>
  <p>
    Your whole archive exports to markdown or JSON — full archived text included — any
    time, from <a href="/settings">Settings</a>. Deleting your account deletes your
    saves, lenses, tokens{groups ? ', memberships and what you shared to groups. Groups you own are deleted for every member' : ' — everything tied to you'}.
    You can delete your account at the bottom of <a href="/settings#delete-account">Settings</a>,
    and archived pages that nobody else saved are deleted along with it.
  </p>
</Layout>
